{"name":"Half Moon Bay Blush","hex":"#cda894","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"half","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of two usually roughly equal parts into which anything may be divided, or considered as divided.","One of two equal periods into which a game is divided."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Consisting of a half (½, 50%).","Consisting of some indefinite portion resembling a half; approximately a half, whether more or less; partial; imperfect."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/half"},{"term":"moon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Moon (“the Earth's only permanent natural satellite”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any natural satellite of a planet.","The light received from the Moon; moonlight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moon"},{"term":"bay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Batuley."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A body of water (especially the sea) contained by a concave shoreline.","A bank or dam to keep back water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moon Bay","blocks":[{"text":"Moon Bay is a bay 7 nautical miles (13 km) wide which recedes 4 nautical miles (7 km) between Edinburgh Hill and Renier Point, on the east side of Livingston Island, in the South Shetland Islands off Antarctica. The glaciers Sopot Ice Piedmont, Iskar, Huron, Struma, Kaliakra, Panega and Debelt feed the bay. Both Half Moon Island and Elemag Reef lie in Moon Bay."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Huron.jpg/330px-Huron.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon_Bay","wikidata":"Q217953"},{"title":"Half Moon Bay, California","blocks":[{"text":"Half Moon Bay is a coastal city in San Mateo County, California, United States, approximately 25 miles south of San Francisco. Its population was 11,795 as of the 2020 census. Immediately north of Half Moon Bay is Pillar Point Harbor and the unincorporated community of Princeton-by-the-Sea. Half Moon Bay is known for Mavericks, a big-wave surf location. It is called Half Moon Bay because of its crescent shape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/2009_aerial_view_of_Pillar_Point_Harbor_and_Bluff.jpg/330px-2009_aerial_view_of_Pillar_Point_Harbor_and_Bluff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Half_Moon_Bay%2C_California","wikidata":"Q983652"},{"title":"One half","blocks":[{"text":"One half is the multiplicative inverse of 2. It is an irreducible fraction with a numerator of 1 and a denominator of 2. It often appears in mathematical equations, recipes and measurements."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_half","wikidata":"Q2114394"},{"title":"Moon","blocks":[{"text":"The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits Earth in an elliptical path, at a distance that varies over the year, averaging around 378,000 kilometers (235,000 mi). It completes an orbit in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period to its orbital period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/330px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon","wikidata":"Q405"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Seal_of_Half_Moon_Bay%2C_California.png/120px-Seal_of_Half_Moon_Bay%2C_California.png?utm_s